Subject: [PATCH] perf(construction): hash the taxonomy_candidates join, and settle
 the evidence tie it exposed

Base revision: 4cd39bf3a ("chore(api): document the construction rate limit at its
measured value") — the live API revision. Applies with `git apply` against that
revision alone; it carries nothing else from the working tree.

WHAT IT CHANGES
Two edits in matchSuppliers, both in one SQL string.

1. `taxonomy_candidates` joined `supplier_specialties` to `requested` on an OR of
   three equalities (farq_spec_id, lower(subcategory), lower(category)). Postgres
   cannot hash an OR join, so it nested-looped the whole specialty directory once
   per requested family. The new `requested_specialty_keys` CTE folds the three
   spellings into one hashable column and the join becomes an equi-join.

   `select distinct` in that CTE is load-bearing, not tidiness. Production has
   families whose farq_spec_id equals their own lower(category) or
   lower(subcategory) — 30 of each were found. Without `distinct` those families
   would match a specialty twice and duplicate a pair the OR form emitted once,
   which would change jsonb_agg array lengths downstream. With it, a specialty
   string equals at most one key per family, so every qualifying pair is produced
   exactly once and the left join to category_map multiplies rows identically.

2. A deterministic tie-break on the `distinct on (supplier_id, item_id)` inside
   `strongest_unfiltered`. Its ORDER BY ended at observed_at, so two specialty
   rows tying on status, confidence AND observed_at left the surviving evidence
   row decided by join order. This is a latent defect that predates the patch:
   any plan change could have altered those excerpts. It is included here because
   the rewrite cannot be proven identical while it stands — without it the rewrite
   moves 3 excerpts, which would read as a regression it did not cause.

MEASURED, ON THIS REVISION
Speed — the five supplier windows an 80-line booklet issues:
  window 1  189 rows  6529 ms -> 814 ms
  window 2  134 rows  5281 ms -> 791 ms
  window 3  172 rows  5582 ms -> 854 ms
  window 4  203 rows  5458 ms -> 841 ms
  window 5  129 rows  1172 ms -> 699 ms
  total SQL  24.02 s -> 4.00 s  (6.01x), every window byte-identical.

Blast radius — the query text is one string and every consumer reaches it, so all
of them were compared, not the one that motivated the change. 51 call shapes were
captured from this revision's own code: 11 item groups (including the families
whose farq_spec_id equals their own lower(category)/lower(subcategory), and the
groups that reach every veto branch inside the rewritten CTE) x 5 caller shapes
(the booklet path with coverage preserved and 12 candidates per family; the
/suppliers/match page path at 3; the include_inferred variant the send gates use;
and the spare-parts path).

  Join rewrite, against pristine + the same tie-break:
    51 of 51 shapes BYTE-IDENTICAL across 16,593 supplier rows — same columns,
    same row order as the query produced it, same match_evidence arrays.

  Whole patch, against what production runs today:
    6 supplier rows of 16,593 differ, in `evidence_excerpt` ALONE.
    Zero change to (item_id, status, confidence), matched_item_ids, evidence
    grades, match scores, coverage counts or supplier ordering.
    Those 6 are rows where two evidence candidates tie on status, confidence and
    observed_at; the excerpt shown becomes the stable one of two equally valid
    strings rather than whichever the planner happened to feed first.

TESTS, on this revision
Measured pristine vs patched, same machine, same database:
  all construction suites   666 pass / 14 fail  BEFORE
                            666 pass / 14 fail  AFTER
  Identical totals and an identical list of failing suites — no test changed
  state. The 14 are pre-existing and unrelated (migration-file text assertions,
  ontology data, supplier_category_map confidences, extraction benchmark, inbox).

The eight suites that actually reach this query all pass on the patched revision:
  boq-catalog-matching 9, boq-directory-matching 15, boq-recovery 3,
  boq-supplier-coverage-sql 1, platform-contracts 12, registry-file-search 8,
  supplier-discovery 12, write-role-gates 6.

diff --git a/api/lib/construction/supabase-repository.js b/api/lib/construction/supabase-repository.js
index 78c657066..1a7288e03 100755
--- a/api/lib/construction/supabase-repository.js
+++ b/api/lib/construction/supabase-repository.js
@@ -1640,6 +1640,31 @@ function createSupabaseConstructionRepository({ connectionString, dispatcher, po
           join construction.suppliers supplier on supplier.id=specialty.supplier_id and supplier.active=true
           where specialty.active=true and specialty.confidence >= 0.85
             and lower(specialty.specialty_id) <> 'general-supply'
+        ), requested_specialty_keys as (
+          /*
+           * The three specialty spellings a requested family answers to, as one
+           * hashable column. taxonomy_candidates used to express this as an OR
+           * of three equalities, which Postgres cannot hash: it nested-looped
+           * the whole supplier_specialties CTE once per requested family. For
+           * the five windows an 80-line booklet issues, that was 24.02s of SQL
+           * against 4.00s here for byte-identical rows.
+           *
+           * distinct is what makes the row counts identical rather than merely
+           * similar. Production has families whose farq_spec_id equals their own
+           * lower(category) or lower(subcategory); without it those families
+           * would match a specialty twice and duplicate the pair the OR form
+           * emitted once. With it, a specialty string equals at most one key per
+           * family, so every qualifying pair is produced exactly once — and the
+           * left join to category_map below multiplies rows the same way in both
+           * forms.
+           */
+          select distinct item_id, key from (
+            select id as item_id, farq_spec_id as key from requested
+            union all
+            select id, lower(subcategory) from requested where subcategory is not null
+            union all
+            select id, lower(category) from requested
+          ) spellings
         ), capability_candidates as (
           select
             capability.supplier_id, capability.item_id, capability.match_status,
@@ -1673,9 +1698,8 @@ function createSupabaseConstructionRepository({ connectionString, dispatcher, po
             specialties.source_observed_at as observed_at,
             requested.farq_spec_id
           from supplier_specialties specialties
-          join requested on specialties.specialty = requested.farq_spec_id
-            or (requested.subcategory is not null and specialties.specialty = lower(requested.subcategory))
-            or specialties.specialty = lower(requested.category)
+          join requested_specialty_keys spelling on spelling.key = specialties.specialty
+          join requested on requested.id = spelling.item_id
           left join category_map on category_map.category in (
             requested.farq_spec_id, lower(coalesce(requested.subcategory, '')), lower(requested.category)
           )
@@ -1901,7 +1925,12 @@ function createSupabaseConstructionRepository({ connectionString, dispatcher, po
               when 'SPECIALTY_INFERRED' then 1
               else 0
             end desc,
-            candidate.confidence desc, candidate.observed_at desc
+            candidate.confidence desc, candidate.observed_at desc,
+            -- Two specialty rows can tie on status, confidence and observed_at,
+            -- which left the surviving evidence row's excerpt decided by join
+            -- order. Break the tie on the evidence's own identity so the winner
+            -- is stable no matter how the planner feeds this CTE.
+            candidate.evidence_kind, candidate.evidence_excerpt, candidate.evidence_url
         ), coverage_ranked as (
           select candidate.*, row_number() over (
             partition by candidate.item_id
